"""        <h2>The Echo <span class="em">🕰️</span></h2>

        <div class="echo-card">
            <p>The listing was spectacular: a city of gold, somewhere inland, where a king powdered himself with gold dust and treasure lay in heaps. El Dorado. On the strength of that promise, thousands of Spanish conquistadors marched into jungles and mountains — through fever, starvation and years of ruin — chasing an advert nobody had fact-checked.</p>
            <p>The gap between promise and reality has rarely been wider. There was no golden city; there was a lake ceremony, exaggerated in the retelling, polished by every teller because polishing it served every teller. Expeditions kept setting out anyway — because they'd already paid so much to believe it, and the more a man has invested in a promise, the harder he'll fight anyone who checks it.</p>
            <p>You, at least, can check. The photos are on your phone, the listing is on the app, and the gap between them is a ten-minute phone call — made tonight, before you've invested a single night more.</p>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="short_history_the_conquistadors.html">🏆 <span>Want the full story? Gold, fever, and the city that never existed…</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <h3>Two bigger questions</h3>
        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>The conquistadors kept believing in El Dorado because they'd paid too much to stop. Where does "I've already invested so much" keep people in bad rooms, bad jobs, bad plans?</li>
            <li>Reviews, photos, listings: we all present the sunny side. What's the most honest advert you've ever seen — and did it work on you?</li>
        </ol>""",
